	abstract = {We demonstrate that substitutional 𝐵 in silicon can migrate even at room temperature and below, stimulated by a high interstitial flux. Once mobile 𝐵 is formed, it migrates for long distances with a diffusivity {\textgreater}5 ×10−13 c⁢m2/s, until it assumes an immobile configuration with a migration length independent of the temperature. This phenomenon is present during secondary ion mass spectrometry (SIMS) analyses of 𝐵 profiles, altering the profile during the analysis itself. These results shed new light on all the data based on SIMS analyses and reported in literature in the last decades.},
